WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.136
Before anything else, picture this.

00:00:02.536 --> 00:00:09.142
Your user table no longer fits on one machine. Sharding splits it, and choosing the shard key is the interview.

00:00:10.342 --> 00:00:11.271
Pause and think.

00:00:11.671 --> 00:00:13.018
What is a hot shard?

00:00:13.418 --> 00:00:20.732
Hold your answer; we will check it at the end. And whenever a new word comes up, we stop for a moment and explain it.

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:27.775
This lesson covers sharding: splitting data across many machines when one machine is not enough.

00:00:27.817 --> 00:00:37.639
New word: sharding. Splitting data across many machines by key. Picture it like this: Splitting a phone book into A–F, G–M, N–Z volumes.

00:00:40.167 --> 00:00:43.011
Imagine a phone book too big for one shelf.

00:00:43.461 --> 00:00:49.672
You split it into volumes: A to F on shelf one, G to M on shelf two, and so on.

00:00:50.122 --> 00:00:54.697
To find a name, you first pick the right shelf, then search only that volume.

00:00:55.147 --> 00:00:59.547
Each shelf is a shard. The rule that picks the shelf is the partition key.

00:00:59.650 --> 00:01:10.993
New word: partition. A network split where machines can't reach each other; also a slice of data (shard). Picture it like this: A storm cutting the phone line between two branches.

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:15.183
Two main ways to split.

00:01:15.633 --> 00:01:23.818
Range partitioning keeps keys in sorted ranges, like A to F. Range scans are easy, but a popular range becomes a hot spot.

00:01:24.268 --> 00:01:30.689
Hash partitioning spreads keys by hash. Load is even, but range scans must ask every shard.

00:01:31.139 --> 00:01:36.897
Many systems combine both: hash on user id, then sort by time inside each shard.

00:01:38.247 --> 00:01:39.606
Here is a hot spot.

00:01:40.056 --> 00:01:44.595
Shards are split by hash, but one celebrity user gets most of the traffic.

00:01:45.045 --> 00:01:48.145
Shard two is overloaded while the others are idle.

00:01:48.595 --> 00:01:55.491
A fix is to split that one hot key across sub keys, like user id plus a random suffix, and merge on read.

00:01:56.841 --> 00:01:57.840
Vocabulary.

00:01:58.290 --> 00:02:07.021
Rebalancing moves shards when nodes are added. Using many more shards than nodes, like a thousand shards on ten machines, makes moves small.

00:02:07.149 --> 00:02:16.797
New word: node. One point in a graph or tree; also one machine in a cluster. Picture it like this: A city on a map, or one computer in a group.

00:02:18.399 --> 00:02:22.149
A router, or coordinator, knows which shard lives where.

00:02:22.599 --> 00:02:29.994
A cross shard query is called scatter gather: send to all, then merge the answers, often with a heap for top k.

00:02:30.033 --> 00:02:42.409
New word: heap. A tree-shaped structure that always gives you the smallest (or largest) item quickly; also the dynamic memory area. Picture it like this: A tournament where the champion is always on top.

00:02:44.059 --> 00:02:49.934
Cross shard transactions are expensive, so choose a partition key that keeps related data together.

00:02:51.284 --> 00:02:58.435
Pick a partition key, watch for hot spots, and plan rebalancing early. See the vector search and object store designs.

00:02:58.432 --> 00:02:59.686
Back to the question.

00:03:00.086 --> 00:03:01.433
What is a hot shard?

00:03:01.833 --> 00:03:06.720
One shard getting far more traffic than the rest, often from a bad key choice.

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:08.791
Quick recap.

00:03:09.191 --> 00:03:10.526
Split data by a key.

00:03:10.926 --> 00:03:13.353
Pick keys that spread load evenly.

00:03:13.753 --> 00:03:16.052
Resharding is costly; plan ahead.
